WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:08.039
So now that our character animation is done and added to our sequence, it's time to create our Cinema Camera actor

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:15.919
so that when the sequence plays that you see through the camera that we're creating and not the player's viewport.

3
00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:20.760
So there are a couple different ways that we could go about this. Now, let's go back to our sequence.

4
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.059
I'm going to roll up my SKM bot.

5
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:29.539
The first and easiest is we could just click this button to create a new camera and set it as the current camera tracks,

6
00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:31.460
which is what we will do.

7
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:37.600
The second option is we could actually create a Cinema Camera actor, so an actual actor in our level,

8
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:42.039
and adjust our settings and then right-click add it to our sequence.

9
00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:48.219
Now where you may want to choose this option of adding the actual Cinema Camera to your level

10
00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:53.899
is a couple different reasons. If there's a very specific look or feel you have for the camera,

11
00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:55.899
so say for example we change it from

12
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:03.340
16 by 9 digital film to IMAX, or maybe we're using various different lens settings for ultra-wide cinematic,

13
00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:10.859
etc. As opposed to just creating a new default camera, you may want to repurpose a camera that's already in your level.

14
00:01:10.859 --> 00:01:14.019
That is absolutely fine. Totally a doable thing.

15
00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:19.819
Again, all you need to do is add the camera to the level, right-click, and then add the actor to the sequence.

16
00:01:19.819 --> 00:01:21.739
So let's go ahead and delete this.

17
00:01:21.739 --> 00:01:24.719
I'm just going to go back down to sequence and click our little camera button.

18
00:01:24.879 --> 00:01:30.920
So now we get our camera actor, and we get our camera cuts track, which is set up and ready to go.

19
00:01:31.079 --> 00:01:34.959
Now one thing to note real quick about my viewport, yours may or may not look different here.

20
00:01:35.239 --> 00:01:41.640
In particular, if you notice that I have the UI here for our timeline, our playback controls,

21
00:01:41.879 --> 00:01:46.879
the actual camera information, and the sequence that we're in.

22
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.000
If yours looks like this, so the default viewport, and you don't have those controls,

23
00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:55.200
that's simply because you're viewing through the default viewport.

24
00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:01.349
So if we go up to our perspective and change this to our cinematic viewport, of course it will change.

25
00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:06.390
We'll go down to our camera cuts track and simply just toggle this view through, and now

26
00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:13.810
we have our viewport. So now we can see our character, we have our playback controls, and we are ready to rock and roll.

27
00:02:14.289 --> 00:02:20.210
All right, so let's go back to our start here, and what we want to do is just build this camera up

28
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.810
to the way that we want to animate it. Now if you notice that if I right click in my viewport,

29
00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:32.050
and I can't move my camera, to move the camera I could, you know, activate my viewport here,

30
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:38.689
use a, if we go to perspective, and we are cinema locked here, but I could use another viewport

31
00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:43.650
and try to adjust it, but I'll show you another and easier way to do this. So instead of

32
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:49.729
selecting our camera cuts track, if we select our cinema camera actor, we're actually viewing

33
00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:55.169
and we're piloting the cinema camera actor. So now I can move the camera and it will record

34
00:02:55.169 --> 00:03:00.689
all of its information. So for this shot, I think I want to have the character kind of just run off

35
00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:06.210
screen, I'll have the camera move with it. Now this is a little bit tighter angle

36
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:11.169
than what I want, so I can select my cinema camera actor from the sequence, go over to our

37
00:03:11.169 --> 00:03:15.889
details panel, and now what I can do is like change my focal length to something like maybe 22

38
00:03:15.889 --> 00:03:22.849
to get us a wider angle, move the camera forward. Now I am using just the standard kind of viewport

39
00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:30.289
movement control, so just right clicking and then W, A, S, and D, and just get my camera into position.

40
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:35.520
All right, so this should be good to go. Same thing as we animated our character,

41
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.000
put out of my cinema camera actor, and under my transform just hit the little plus button

42
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.879
to add a key, and then we'll go all the way to the end of our shot, and what I want to do is

43
00:03:44.879 --> 00:03:51.439
just have the camera just kind of move forward just slightly so we have some movement here,

44
00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:56.159
a dolly shot of sorts. So if I go back to the beginning, you'll see it created my keyframe,

45
00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:02.659
we'll hit play, now we've got that camera movement following the character as he runs and jumps.

46
00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:09.300
Now quick thing, I like the slow down movement here, but because we're opening the shot just

47
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.819
kind of out of the gate, you may or may not want to change the opening keyframe here to linear,

48
00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:20.819
now if we go back you'll see that the motion just starts the camera, and then it will slow down

49
00:04:20.819 --> 00:04:27.990
towards the end. Perfect, I think that is good to go. All right, so the last thing that we need to do

50
00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.029
is you'll notice that the character is out of focus and the background is in super focus,

51
00:04:33.029 --> 00:04:37.670
this is not what we want. So what I'm going to do is actually select my cinema camera actor,

52
00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:43.670
and in the focus settings I'm going to click the draw, debug, focus settings, I'll kind of move

53
00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:49.079
this out a little bit, the focus plane, and you'll notice it's way off screen, but if I left click and

54
00:04:49.079 --> 00:04:54.600
drag, see it'll bring it into focus where camera is actually focused. You could do this

55
00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.560
manually or you could click the little eyedropper and select the character, you'll see that it

56
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:04.519
snaps, now in this case I'm just going to push it just a little bit forward. All right,

57
00:05:04.519 --> 00:05:09.720
so now if I turn off my draw, debug, focus plane, you see now the background is blurred

58
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.279
and the character is more in focus. Now we could go into more depth on how to

59
00:05:14.279 --> 00:05:19.480
track this so that it always stays in focus on the character, this is a simple shot, not super

60
00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:25.000
concerned about it. One other thing that you may want to change is the depth of field, so right

61
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:33.300
now our current aperture is set to 2.8. So if I decrease this to say something like 1.2,

62
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:38.339
you'll notice that it gets more fuzzy. Now if I increase the aperture to say something like 22,

63
00:05:38.339 --> 00:05:43.939
you'll see that most of the scene is in focus. So I'm going to undo that, go back to our 2.8,

64
00:05:43.939 --> 00:05:49.060
because I do like the cinematic feel of this look, but just know that you can change that

65
00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:54.660
setting to get more or less focus of your elements. All right, go back to our beginning

66
00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:02.550
here, double check that this is in focus, our character moves, runs, jumps, looks great.

67
00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:08.310
All right, perfect. Save our sequence, and so now we have our camera and our character

68
00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:11.829
all nested within our sequence, and we're ready to move on to the next part.
